    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a dish that eats like it came out of a restaurant kitchen. The beans stretch a big cut of pork across 14 servings without watering anything down.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy the whole pork shoulder, not the pre-cubed stew meat.\u003c/strong\u003e Cubing it yourself takes ten minutes with a sharp knife and saves real money per pound.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eChipotles in adobo are the cheapest big flavor in the store.\u003c/strong\u003e A couple of small cans season this whole batch. Freeze what you do not use in a flat bag and break off pieces later.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eOrange juice from a carton is fine here.\u003c/strong\u003e You are cooking it down, not drinking it, so buy the store brand and use the rest for breakfast.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned cannellini beans go in at the end.\u003c/strong\u003e Added late they hold their shape and absorb the sauce. Added early they turn to paste, and paste is not what you want."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cube the pork shoulder, pat it dry, season generously with salt and black pepper, and brown it hard in the olive oil in a heavy pot, in batches so it sears rather than steams.",
                    "Cook the diced onion in the rendered fat until soft, then add the minced garlic, the ground cumin, ground coriander and ground allspice and stir for a minute.",
                    "Stir in the tomato paste and let it darken against the bottom of the pot for two minutes. That step is what separates a deep sauce from a flat one.",
                    "Add the chopped chipotles in adobo, the orange juice and the chicken broth, scrape the pot clean, and return the pork along with the bay leaves and dried oregano.",
                    "Cover and braise gently for about 3 hours, until the pork shreds under a fork and the liquid has reduced into a thick sauce.",
                    "Stir in the drained cannellini beans, simmer 15 more minutes so they take on the sauce, and pull out the bay leaves before portioning."
                ],
